<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Matsue Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Matsue</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of Izumo, ruled from the lakeside castle of Matsue, whose lord Matsudaira Harusato was one of Japan's great tea masters.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izumo-province">Izumo</a> and Oki to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horio-yoshiharu">Horio Yoshiharu</a> in 1600. The Horio first used the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-clan">Amago</a> fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gassantoda-castle">Gassan-Toda</a>, then built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> castle on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-shinji">Lake Shinji</a>, finished in 1611.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Horio line died out in 1633 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyogoku-clan">Kyogoku</a> held the domain briefly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-naomasa">Matsudaira Naomasa</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>, received it in 1638 with about 186,000 koku. His descendants ruled until 1871. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-harusato">Matsudaira Harusato</a>, known as Fumai, restored the domain's finances in the late 18th century and became a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master and collector. The domain produced iron by the tatara method, ginseng and wax.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The domain submitted to the Meiji government and was abolished in 1871, becoming part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimane-prefecture">Shimane Prefecture</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsue">Matsue</a> castle's original keep survives as a national treasure.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/matsue-domain.jpg" alt="Matsue Domain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:150321_Matsue_Castle_Matsue_Shimane_pref_Japan01bs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Matsue</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Horio Yoshiharu, Matsudaira Naomasa, Matsudaira Harusato</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Izumo Domain</dd></dl></aside></div>
